{
  "review": {
    "score": 0.72,
    "summary": "The paper introduces a simple, reusable zero-shot prompting method: ask an LLM to devise a plan, carry it out step by step, and, in PS+, extract relevant variables and calculate intermediate results. On ten reasoning datasets, the reported GPT-3 results generally improve on Zero-shot-CoT; PS+ is also competitive with few-shot CoT on arithmetic tasks. The method is practical and supported by broad benchmark comparisons, though the evidence inspected is limited to one model family and does not establish how well the approach generalizes beyond the tested settings.",
    "observations": [
      {
        "kind": "strength",
        "claim": "The prompt is clearly specified, and the authors compare variants that add planning and calculation instructions rather than relying on an underspecified method description.",
        "page": 3,
        "quote": "Let’s ﬁrst understand the problem and devise a plan to solve the problem. Then, let’s carry out the plan and solve the problem step by step."
      },
      {
        "kind": "strength",
        "claim": "The reported results support gains over Zero-shot-CoT across the arithmetic benchmarks: PS+ improves on all six, while the tables also show improvements on the two commonsense and two symbolic datasets.",
        "page": 6,
        "quote": "PS+ prompting improves the accuracy over Zero-shot CoT by at least 5% for all datasets except GSM8K which sees a 2.9% improvement."
      },
      {
        "kind": "strength",
        "claim": "The arithmetic results provide a concrete, qualified comparison with eight-shot Manual-CoT: PS+ has a slightly lower reported average, rather than matching or exceeding it outright.",
        "page": 6,
        "quote": "PS+ prompting yields an average accuracy (76.7%) slightly lower than Manual-CoT (77.6%)"
      },
      {
        "kind": "limitation",
        "claim": "The error analysis suggests PS+ reduces calculation and missing-step errors in the sampled GSM8K cases, but semantic errors remain at the same reported rate as Zero-shot-CoT. The percentages are reported over a 100-problem sample: the 46 incorrect answers are the cases classified across the three error types, not a conflicting denominator.",
        "page": 7,
        "quote": "Zero-shot-PS+ 5% 7% 27%"
      }
    ],
    "unresolved_questions": [
      "Do the reported gains hold with other model families, model versions, or prompt settings? The inspected implementation results use GPT-3 text-davinci-003, with text-davinci-002 results referred to in the appendix.",
      "How sensitive are the findings to sampling and evaluation variation? The excerpts report accuracy comparisons and a 100-problem error analysis but do not provide uncertainty estimates.",
      "What limitations or later discussion are given elsewhere in the paper? Only the first seven PDF pages were inspected; the remaining 17 pages were not."
    ]
  },
  "ledger": [
    {
      "check_id": "C1",
      "status": "supported",
      "finding": "The inspected methods and results specify PS/PS+, ten datasets, GPT-3 text-davinci-003, temperature 0 for greedy decoding, and baseline comparisons. Tables report gains over Zero-shot-CoT across the listed datasets; PS+ is compared with few-shot CoT, including an eight-demonstration arithmetic setting.",
      "support": [
        "E3",
        "E4",
        "E5",
        "E6",
        "E7"
      ],
      "counterevidence": [],
      "unknown": "The other 17 PDF pages were not inspected, including the appendix results and discussion."
    },
    {
      "check_id": "C2",
      "status": "supported",
      "finding": "Tables report PS+ outperforming Zero-shot-CoT on all six arithmetic datasets and on the two commonsense and two symbolic datasets. The reported arithmetic average is 76.7% for PS+ versus 77.6% for Manual-CoT, which the text describes as slightly lower. The error analysis samples 100 GSM8K problems; 46, 43, and 39 were incorrect under Zero-shot-CoT, PS, and PS+ respectively. The 7%, 12%, and 27% error categories for Zero-shot-CoT sum to its 46 incorrect cases, so the apparent denominator discrepancy is clarified by the observed text.",
      "support": [
        "E6",
        "E7",
        "E8"
      ],
      "counterevidence": [],
      "unknown": "The observed excerpts do not establish uncertainty estimates or independently verify the reported experiments."
    },
    {
      "check_id": "C3",
      "status": "unknown",
      "finding": "The inspected excerpts do not cover the full discussion or limitations material, so no conclusion about their absence or content is warranted.",
      "support": [],
      "counterevidence": [],
      "unknown": "The remaining 17 PDF pages, including any discussion or limitations, were not inspected."
    }
  ],
  "changes": "Updated the assessment after inspecting the main results and error-analysis tables. Replaced the previously questionable quotation with exact observed text and clarified the error-analysis denominator: the 100 sampled problems include 46 Zero-shot-CoT errors, whose reported categories sum to 46%. The concern that headline comparisons were unsupported is resolved by the newly observed tables; uncertainty about uninspected pages and generalization remains."
}
